a linear regression over the
last three months of data.

00:26:13.254 --> 00:26:15.420
And then we're going to
solve that linear regression

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:16.860
for the next couple of months.

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:18.900
And what that's going
to do is predict

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:23.950
the current month and the
next month's billing data.

00:26:23.950 --> 00:26:26.280
So we'll see what
that looks like here.

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:28.899
VIC IGLESIAS: How many lines
of code was that in the query?

00:26:28.899 --> 00:26:29.940
IAN LEWIS: In this query?

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:31.942
This one is 83 lines of code.

00:26:31.942 --> 00:26:33.150
VIC IGLESIAS: That's not bad.

00:26:33.150 --> 00:26:34.040
That's not bad.

00:26:34.040 --> 00:26:36.150
IAN LEWIS: What we
get out of this is,

00:26:36.150 --> 00:26:40.840
we get these three fields
from the SQL query.

00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:43.540
And those fields get
translated into the fields that

00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:45.200
come into my report, right?

00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:49.060
So Data Studio knows,
from the Custom Query,

00:26:49.060 --> 00:26:50.890
what the output fields are.

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:53.974
And then we can use those output
fields to generate our report.

00:26:53.974 --> 00:26:54.640
So here we have.

00:26:54.640 --> 00:26:56.860
A future balance and a
balance field and I've

00:26:56.860 --> 00:26:58.390
separated those two.

00:26:58.390 --> 00:27:01.810
But basically, the
past months will

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:03.580
have data in the balance field.

00:27:03.580 --> 00:27:06.160
And the future months will have
the data in the future months

00:27:06.160 --> 00:27:06.850
field.

00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:09.010
And I'll show you
why I did that,

00:27:09.010 --> 00:27:11.722
but it has to do with
the report itself.

00:27:11.722 --> 00:27:13.180
And then I'm just
going to add that

00:27:13.180 --> 00:27:15.480
to my report as a data source.

00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:17.570
And then in here,
in the dimension,

00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:20.050
I can select from the data
that came back from my Custom

00:27:20.050 --> 00:27:25.340
Queries So here I'm going to
select Month as my dimension.

00:27:25.340 --> 00:27:29.549
And as my metric I'm going
to select Balance here.

00:27:29.549 --> 00:27:31.090
And so, you can see
that I've created

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:36.590
a bar graph for the past
three months of billing data.

00:27:36.590 --> 00:27:40.140
So these are my costs for
the last three months.

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:42.021
And so, I'll go back.

00:27:42.021 --> 00:27:44.520
And what you can do is you can
actually add multiple metrics

00:27:44.520 --> 00:27:45.730
to a lot of these graphs.

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:47.670
So what I'm going to do
here is add another metric.

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:49.753
And this time I'm going
to add the future balance.

00:27:52.090 --> 00:27:54.990
So normally what you
would do in these cases

00:27:54.990 --> 00:27:56.400
is multiple metrics
would show up

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:58.700
as bar graphs that sit
next to each other,

00:27:58.700 --> 00:28:04.007
but because these are mutually
exclusive for each month they

00:28:04.007 --> 00:28:05.590
show up with big
spaces in the middle.

00:28:05.590 --> 00:28:07.090
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:28:07.090 --> 00:28:08.970
to do a little bit
of a hack with this.

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:11.654
I'm going to change
it to multiple colors.

00:28:11.654 --> 00:28:13.320
And then I'm going
actually stack these.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:16.620
And since they stack
on basically nothing,

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:19.360
they kind of look like
they're next to each other.

00:28:19.360 --> 00:28:21.990
So this is a little
bit of a display hack,

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:25.440
but you can see that
I did that so that I

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:29.220
can put the past months in blue
and the future months in red

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:30.090
here.

00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:31.830
And so this is actually
the current month

00:28:31.830 --> 00:28:32.530
and the next month.

00:28:32.530 --> 00:28:34.488
So I don't actually have
billing data for this,

00:28:34.488 --> 00:28:37.680
but I'm actually extrapolating
from my past data, what

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:39.230
my future data might look like.

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:42.280
So you can see that my
past data is trending down.

00:28:42.280 --> 00:28:46.017
So we've extrapolated out that
the billing data will go down

00:28:46.017 --> 00:28:47.850
a little bit over the
next couple of months.

00:28:51.859 --> 00:28:52.400
That's great.

00:28:52.400 --> 00:28:56.570
We can predict the future
a little bit and maybe ease

00:28:56.570 --> 00:28:59.720
our financial officers minds'
a little bit about how much

00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:02.520
we're spending.

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:04.590
But the last graph
that I want to do here

00:29:04.590 --> 00:29:10.770
is what really illustrates
the power of using BigQuery,

00:29:10.770 --> 00:29:13.170
exporting your billing
data to BigQuery,

00:29:13.170 --> 00:29:16.830
because BigQuery can be,
you can put any type of data

00:29:16.830 --> 00:29:18.720
into BigQuery table.

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:22.530
And likely you already have
a lot of business data there.

00:29:22.530 --> 00:29:24.990
Which we can do is actually
combine our billing data

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:27.870
with data that we know
about our application

00:29:27.870 --> 00:29:32.940
in order to create much better
and more richer metrics.

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:34.680
So here what I'm
going to do is I'm

00:29:34.680 --> 00:29:42.280
going to create another data
source using a Custom Query.

00:29:42.280 --> 00:29:47.062
And I'm going to use a slightly
less complicated query.

00:29:47.062 --> 00:29:48.770
VIC IGLESIAS: It's
only 82 lines of code.

00:29:48.770 --> 00:29:49.430
I know.

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:52.560
IAN LEWIS: It's actually only
17 lines of code, this one.

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:56.810
But this one is actually
a really simple query.

00:29:56.810 --> 00:30:00.590
And I'll go ahead
and connect that in.

00:30:00.590 --> 00:30:03.470
But what I'm going to do
is, since my application

00:30:03.470 --> 00:30:08.010
is a, that's interesting.

00:30:08.010 --> 00:30:09.750
In with the wrong fields there.

00:30:12.650 --> 00:30:15.370
I didn't quite copy
and paste it properly.

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:15.870
There we go.

00:30:18.630 --> 00:30:21.520
So what I'm doing
here is my application

00:30:21.520 --> 00:30:22.720
is a web application, right?

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:26.080
So I'm using Google Analytics.

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:29.030
And I have Google
Analytics Premium

00:30:29.030 --> 00:30:32.890
so I'm actually exporting that
data, the analytics data too

00:30:32.890 --> 00:30:34.220
BigQuery.

00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:36.640
So in this case, I'm just
using my Analytics data.

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:39.720
I'm going to go
ahead and apply this.

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:45.630
To join that Analytics table
with my BigQuery table.

00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:51.610
And then generate, essentially,
a cost per unique user.

00:30:51.610 --> 00:30:54.100
And so, what I'm doing
here is I get out

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:56.560
a couple of fields, the date
and the cost per unique user.

00:30:56.560 --> 00:31:00.250
So I'll add that to the report.

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:03.220
And I'll show you
the graph first,

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:05.380
but then we can look at
what the query is doing.

00:31:05.380 --> 00:31:09.822
And I'll explain a little bit
about what the query is doing.

00:31:09.822 --> 00:31:11.280
VIC IGLESIAS: One
thing to note is,

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:15.040
despite all of our data sources
here coming from BigQuery,

00:31:15.040 --> 00:31:16.670
there are a bunch
of other sources

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:19.150
you can use like
just a regular sheet.

00:31:19.150 --> 00:31:21.100
If you've got a sheet
with a bunch of data

00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:23.080
that you need to merge
in with some BigQuery,

00:31:23.080 --> 00:31:24.080
you can do that as well.

00:31:24.080 --> 00:31:26.200
Not everything has to
be coming from BigQuery.

00:31:26.200 --> 00:31:27.310
IAN LEWIS: Yeah, right.

00:31:27.310 --> 00:31:31.060
So for a single
report you can only

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:33.760
choose one single data source.

00:31:33.760 --> 00:31:36.430
For inside a report, you
can have multiple graphs

00:31:36.430 --> 00:31:39.130
and each one can come from
different data sources.

00:31:39.130 --> 00:31:42.040
But for a single graph,
you need to have,

00:31:42.040 --> 00:31:44.140
you can only associate
one data source.

00:31:44.140 --> 00:31:46.720
So generally, if you want to
combine multiple data sources

00:31:46.720 --> 00:31:49.720
into a single graph, then
the best way to do that

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:52.120
is with multiple tables
and then joining them

00:31:52.120 --> 00:31:53.830
with a Custom Query.

00:31:53.830 --> 00:31:55.690
And that's exactly
what I did here.

00:31:55.690 --> 00:32:01.920
So we'll take a look at what
this particular query is doing.

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:06.660
What we're doing here exactly is
we're taking our billing data,

00:32:06.660 --> 00:32:09.200
and this is my billing
table, and then

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:13.370
we're doing a joint on that to
our Google Analytics sessions

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:14.910
table.

00:32:14.910 --> 00:32:19.490
And when we do that,
we can extract out

00:32:19.490 --> 00:32:22.370
the distinct visitor IDs.

00:32:22.370 --> 00:32:26.570
So this essentially gives
us a unique user everyday.

00:32:26.570 --> 00:32:27.070
Yeah.

00:32:27.070 --> 00:32:28.810
And we're grouping that by date.

00:32:28.810 --> 00:32:31.060
And then, we join
that with the tables.

00:32:31.060 --> 00:32:33.760
And then, up at
the top here we're

00:32:33.760 --> 00:32:37.180
dividing the cost by the
number of unique users

00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:40.230
to get the costs
per unique user.

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:42.350
So we can how that
goes over time.

00:32:42.350 --> 00:32:47.820
And we can see that we
have some spiky days where

00:32:47.820 --> 00:32:49.980
our cost per unique user
goes up really high.

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:53.550
So we can investigate why
that might be and maybe

00:32:53.550 --> 00:32:57.510
find out why our
application is becoming

00:32:57.510 --> 00:33:00.550
inefficient on those days.

00:33:00.550 --> 00:33:02.800
We can also combine
things with, just

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:05.800
like Google Analytics
but data that

00:33:05.800 --> 00:33:07.930
happens to be coming
from your application.

00:33:07.930 --> 00:33:10.240
Maybe you have log data
that's already created

00:33:10.240 --> 00:33:11.920
that you can join it to.

00:33:11.920 --> 00:33:14.140
Or maybe you have
some other type

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:17.020
of data about how
many users you have

00:33:17.020 --> 00:33:22.970
or the lifetime revenue that
you get from those users.

00:33:22.970 --> 00:33:25.330
And you of compare
that to how much

00:33:25.330 --> 00:33:31.240
you're spending for your
resources in the Cloud.

00:33:31.240 --> 00:33:33.790
So this is very, very
powerful to actually get

00:33:33.790 --> 00:33:36.960
a much more holistic
view about how

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:38.490
you're spending your resources.

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:41.520
Not just, I'm spending x
number of dollars here,

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:45.741
x number of dollars here,
but actually per user

00:33:45.741 --> 00:33:46.740
how much you're costing.

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:49.860
So that in the future say, if
you have an application that

00:33:49.860 --> 00:33:51.750
has 5,000 users
right now, but what

00:33:51.750 --> 00:33:55.290
happens if you have 10,000
users or 100,000 users?

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:57.840
In those cases,
you can extrapolate

00:33:57.840 --> 00:34:02.190
how much your application would
cost as your application scales

00:34:02.190 --> 00:34:02.910
into the future.

00:34:06.850 --> 00:34:09.400
And this is also
really good because say

00:34:09.400 --> 00:34:12.130
if you are using
multiple Clouds,

00:34:12.130 --> 00:34:15.380
who happens to be using multiple
Clouds, GCP plus Amazon?

00:34:15.380 --> 00:34:16.810
So a lot of you, right?

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:19.540
So one of those things that
you could do with this is say,

00:34:19.540 --> 00:34:24.040
you get your billing
data, you export it

00:34:24.040 --> 00:34:26.170
to billing, your
GCP billing data,

00:34:26.170 --> 00:34:30.940
you export that to BigQuery, you
get your CSV dump from Amazon,

00:34:30.940 --> 00:34:33.949
you put that into BigQuery
because BigQuery is awesome.

00:34:33.949 --> 00:34:37.659
And then, you put
those two together

00:34:37.659 --> 00:34:41.949
and you can of look at your cost
per user say, for each Cloud.

00:34:41.949 --> 00:34:46.150
So say, GCP versus
another Cloud.

00:34:46.150 --> 00:34:49.960
What's the cost per user for
each of those clouds, right?

00:34:49.960 --> 00:34:52.630
And you can see as your
application scales up and gets

00:34:52.630 --> 00:34:57.040
more users, where you're
actually syncing those costs

00:34:57.040 --> 00:35:01.520
and where the cost
is more efficient.

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:05.730
So I know that you guys don't
want to pay us more money,

00:35:05.730 --> 00:35:09.300
but the way to be happy, for
everyone to be happy maybe,

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:14.840
is for everyone
to realize perhaps

00:35:14.840 --> 00:35:17.390
which Clouds have the
best cost per user

00:35:17.390 --> 00:35:23.577
and move those workloads
hopefully to GCP.

00:35:23.577 --> 00:35:25.910
VIC IGLESIAS: So what we've
shown you today is basically

00:35:25.910 --> 00:35:27.620
how to look at your
bill and pay us

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:30.260
less money, which is
totally great for us

00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:32.630
we want that to happen.

00:35:32.630 --> 00:35:35.750
I think all this power
and these specific queries

00:35:35.750 --> 00:35:38.539
are not important as much
as the conceptual pieces

00:35:38.539 --> 00:35:40.580
that we've shown you, the
fact that you can bring

00:35:40.580 --> 00:35:42.410
different data
sources in the fact

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:45.350
that you can chop up your
bill in different ways,

00:35:45.350 --> 00:35:48.770
and especially that you can
use Data Studio to make really

00:35:48.770 --> 00:35:52.490
easy dashboards
that you can present

00:35:52.490 --> 00:35:54.475
a holistic view of your bill.

00:35:54.475 --> 00:35:56.600
And depending on what
questions you're being asked,

00:35:56.600 --> 00:36:01.140
you can answer those really
easily with Data Studio.

00:36:01.140 --> 00:36:03.020
So that's about it
for our presentation.

